/**
 * Commercial vertical — restaurants and food service. C-07 verified.
 *
 * REGULATORY SPINE, verified Aug 2026:
 *  - 4 TAC §7.146(c): a licensee must provide a pest control sign at least 48
 *    hours before a planned indoor treatment at, among others, a
 *    FOOD-PROCESSING ESTABLISHMENT — to the chief administrator, IPM
 *    Coordinator or building manager. §7.148(b) puts the POSTING duty on them.
 *  - A restaurant is also a WORKPLACE under §7.146(b)/§7.147(d): sign and
 *    consumer information sheet to the employer or building manager, employer
 *    posts >=48h ahead in an area of common access employees are most likely to
 *    see, sheet available on request during normal business hours.
 *  - §7.144: pest control use records kept 2 years, incl. pesticide names AND
 *    EPA registration numbers, amounts, mixing rate, target pest, date,
 *    applicator name + licence number, business TPCL. Available to TDA on
 *    written or verbal request. THIS is the record an inspector wants.
 *  - §7.145(b): business name, licence number, phone, address on every invoice.
 *  - Health & Safety Code §341.011(3): a public health nuisance includes "a
 *    restaurant, food market, bakery, other place of business, or vehicle in
 *    which food is prepared, packed, stored, transported, sold, or served to
 *    the public and that is not constantly maintained in a sanitary condition."
 *    §341.011(1): "a condition or place that is a breeding place for flies and
 *    that is in a populous area."
 *
 * GERMAN COCKROACH (AgriLife ENTO-037) — the defining pest. Does NOT live
 * outdoors in Texas; carried in. 75% of life in cracks 1/32-3/16 in. Female
 * carries ootheca to hatch. 30-40 nymphs/capsule, nymph->adult 50-60 days,
 * optimum ~80F, a generation in ~100 days. Bug bombs do not penetrate cracks.
 * Daytime sightings indicate a large population. Dispersal beyond the kitchen
 * is a severity indicator. Public-health pest; allergen/asthma link (NIEHS);
 * HEPA vacuum. Baits primary: >=1 placement within 1-2 ft of every suspected
 * harbourage, many small pea-sized placements beat few large; rotate active
 * ingredient if no result at 2-3 weeks. Boric acid effective, applied so
 * lightly residue is barely visible. Hair dryer to flush harbourage.
 *
 * AMERICAN COCKROACH (ENTO-037): largest roach in Texas, 1.5-2 in, reddish
 * brown with tan/light-yellow banding outlining the pronotum, both sexes fly.
 * Outdoor/commercial-building insect: compost, ground cover, hollow trees,
 * mulch, stumps, woodpiles, SEWER MANHOLES, underground water meters; "one of
 * the most common cockroaches in sewer systems". Enters via poorly sealed
 * doors, roof soffits and FLOOR DRAINS. Prevention verbatim: "Keep P-traps
 * filled with water to create a barrier between the sewer and the home or
 * business." "Check the threshold seals under doors and ensure that roof
 * soffits are screened." Development ~600 days; adults 1-2 years.
 * ORIENTAL COCKROACH: dark brown/black, ~1.25 in, does not fly, prefers cooler
 * ground-level sites, also uses floor drains.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Commercial', path: '/commercial/' },
  { name: 'Restaurants & food service', path: '/commercial/restaurants/' },
];

const quickAnswer =
  'Food service pest control across the Permian Basin, from a licensed owner-operator. Two facts shape almost every job: German cockroaches are carried in rather than sprayed in, and a large reddish roach at night is nearly always a drain or door problem rather than an infestation. The service record we keep under 4 TAC §7.144 is the document your inspector actually wants to see.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What record will my health inspector ask for?',
    a: 'The service record, and it is more detailed than most operators volunteer. Under 4 TAC §7.144 pest control use records are kept for two years and must include the service address, the pesticides used with their EPA registration numbers, total amounts applied, devices used, the mixing rate, the target pest or purpose, the date of application, and the applicator’s name and licence number alongside the business licence number. Ask for copies for your own file — an inspector wants to see a documented programme, not a receipt.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the 48-hour notice rule apply to a restaurant?',
    a: 'Yes, on two separate footings. A restaurant is a workplace under 4 TAC §7.146(b), and a food-processing establishment is named specifically in §7.146(c). Either way we must provide a pest control sign at least 48 hours before a planned indoor treatment — and under §7.148(b) the employer or building manager, not us, must post it in an area of common access at least 48 hours ahead, and make the consumer information sheet available to anyone working in the building who asks.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does spraying not fix German cockroaches?',
    a: 'Because of where they live and how they breed. AgriLife records that German cockroaches spend about seventy-five percent of their lives in crevices between a thirty-second and three-sixteenths of an inch wide, and that total-release aerosols generally do not eliminate them because they do not penetrate those cracks. On top of that, the female carries the egg capsule until the nymphs are ready to emerge, so surface insecticide largely misses the next generation. Correctly placed gel bait, sanitation and crevice sealing are the treatment; the spray is the least effective part.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I have a big reddish roach in the dining room at night. Is my kitchen infested?',
    a: 'Almost certainly not, and this is the most useful distinction in food service. A large reddish-brown roach an inch and a half to two inches long, with tan banding outlining the shield behind the head, is an American cockroach — an outdoor and sewer insect that AgriLife describes as one of the most common cockroaches in sewer systems. It came in through a floor drain, a poorly sealed door threshold or a roof soffit. That is a plumbing and exclusion problem. A half-inch light brown roach with two dark stripes in the kitchen is a German cockroach and a genuine infestation. They are completely different jobs.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the single cheapest thing I can do?',
    a: 'Keep the P-traps filled. AgriLife’s prevention guidance is verbatim: keep P-traps filled with water to create a barrier between the sewer and the home or business. A floor drain that has dried out — in a walk-in, a mop sink, an unused prep area — is an open pipe from the sewer into your building, and American and oriental cockroaches use floor drains as common points of entry. Running water down every drain weekly costs nothing.',
  },
