---

<BaseLayout
  title="Restaurant Pest Control | Permian Basin"
  description="Food service pest control with the documentation an inspector asks for. German cockroach programmes, drain and door exclusion, out-of-hours service."
  path="/commercial/restaurants/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Commercial · restaurants &amp; food service</p>
      <h1>Restaurants and food service</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Food service is the category where pest control stops being about comfort and starts being about
          your licence to operate. It is also the category where the two most common calls turn out to be
          completely different problems that look similar to everybody except an entomologist.
        </p>
        <p>
          So this page starts with that distinction, because getting it right is the difference between a
          plumbing repair and a treatment programme.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Two roaches, two entirely different problems</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>The half-inch one with two stripes.</strong> Light brown, half to five-eighths of an
          inch, with two dark parallel stripes running down the shield behind the head. That is a
          <strong>German cockroach</strong>, and it is a genuine infestation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The big reddish one at night.</strong> An inch and a half to two inches — the largest
          cockroach in Texas — reddish brown with tan to light-yellow banding outlining the pronotum, and
          it flies. That is an <strong>American cockroach</strong>, and it is almost certainly a wanderer
          rather than a resident.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why the distinction is so sharp.</strong> AgriLife's ENTO-037 states that German
          cockroaches "do not live outdoors in Texas" and "spend their lives indoors." They are carried in
          — in cartons, in produce deliveries, in second-hand equipment, in a supplier's crate. Perimeter
          treatment does nothing about them because there is no perimeter population.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>American cockroaches are the opposite.</strong> They are outdoor and commercial-building
          insects, living in compost, ground cover, hollow trees, mulch, old stumps, woodpiles, sewer
          manholes and underground water meters — and AgriLife calls them one of the most common
          cockroaches in sewer systems. They "live mostly outdoors but may move indoors in search of food
          or water," entering through poorly sealed doors, roof soffits and floor drains.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>So one is a treatment programme and the other is a maintenance job.</strong> Confusing
          them means either spending on a spray programme aimed at an animal that is walking in through a
          dry drain, or dismissing a genuine German cockroach problem as "just one roach."
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>There is a third worth knowing.</strong> The oriental cockroach — dark brown to black,
          around an inch and a quarter, does not fly, wings short in both sexes — prefers cooler
          ground-level sites like basements, crawl spaces and wet areas, and also uses floor drains to get
          in. Same category of problem as the American.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Drains, doors and soffits — the cheap half of the job</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          AgriLife's prevention guidance for the large peridomestic roaches is three sentences long and
          costs almost nothing to act on.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>"Keep P-traps filled with water to create a barrier between the sewer and the home or
          business."</strong> This is the single highest-value item on this page. A floor drain that has
          dried out is an open pipe from the sewer into your building. The drains that dry are the ones
          nobody uses — in a walk-in, under a three-compartment sink that is out of service, in a mop
          closet, in a prep area that changed use. Running water down every drain weekly, or fitting trap
          primers where a drain is chronically dry, closes the route entirely.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>"Check the threshold seals under doors."</strong> Back doors in food service take
          enormous punishment, get propped open during deliveries and service, and the sweep is usually the
          first thing to fail. A door that latches perfectly can still have a measurable gap at the bottom.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>"Ensure that roof soffits are screened."</strong> Under-inspected everywhere and
          particularly on older buildings, where a torn or missing soffit screen is an open route into the
          ceiling void.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Add the ones specific to this region.</strong> Dumpster placement and the ground under it
          — grease and food residue in gravel or caliche supports a permanent population a few yards from
          your back door. Grease trap condition, and how often it is actually pumped. Standing water anywhere on the lot, which in a semi-arid
          climate is the only reliable moisture for a hundred feet and draws everything.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And flies.</strong> Texas Health and Safety Code §341.011(1) makes a condition or place
          that is a breeding place for flies, in a populous area, a public health nuisance in its own
          right. Drain flies in particular are a biofilm problem living inside the drain itself rather than an airborne
          problem in the room, and cleaning the biofilm off the drain wall is the fix, rather than spraying the
          air above it or pouring something down and hoping.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>A German cockroach programme in a kitchen</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          AgriLife's stated hierarchy is inspection, sanitation, exclusion, then low-toxicity insecticides.
          In a commercial kitchen that plays out specifically.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Inspection and monitoring.</strong> Sticky-card monitors along walls and equipment
          edges, which find harbourage rather than guessing at it and give you a reading over time instead
          of an argument. A hair dryer is a legitimate inspection tool here — warm air flushes roaches out
          of harbourage so you can see where they actually are.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Where they actually are.</strong> Dark shelter near moisture and food. Under sinks,
          behind plumbing, behind and under appliances, in cabinet voids, behind coving and baseboards, in
          motor housings and equipment legs, in the seams of stainless where it meets the wall. Warm
          equipment is a particular draw — optimum development temperature is around eighty degrees, which
          describes the space behind a dish machine rather well.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Sanitation and moisture, without the lecture.</strong> Cleaning under and behind
          equipment where residues accumulate, and fixing leaks — AgriLife names removing moisture sources
          explicitly. Most food service premises are run properly and still have three or four specific
          points where residue and moisture meet, and those points are the job.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Vacuuming with a HEPA filter</strong> — AgriLife specifies HEPA because cockroach
          harbourages contain allergens. It removes the population and its food residues at once and gives
          an immediate reduction that bait takes weeks to match.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Sealing the crevices.</strong> Cabinet voids, penetrations for plumbing and electrical,
          gaps behind splashbacks, cracked coving. In a kitchen this is also a cleanability improvement, so
          it tends to pay for itself twice.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Bait, placed properly.</strong> At least one placement within one to two feet of every
          suspected harbourage, with many small pea-sized placements rather than a few large ones. If the
          population has not responded within two to three weeks, rotate to a different bait with a new
          active ingredient rather than repeating. Boric acid dust remains highly effective, applied so
          lightly the residue is barely visible.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Two readings that tell you how bad it is.</strong> Daytime sightings — AgriLife notes
          that seeing cockroaches during the day usually means there are a lot of them around. And
          dispersal: as a population grows and food runs short they spread out of the kitchen into dining
          areas, offices and storage. A report from front of house is a severity indicator rather than a
          location detail, and it changes the scope of the job.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And two things to stop your staff doing.</strong> Setting off foggers, which AgriLife
          says do not work and which scatter the population. And spraying retail product over bait
          placements — many are repellent and will drive roaches away from the bait that was about to kill
          them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The paperwork, and why it is the deliverable</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          On residential work the deliverable is the outcome. In food service the documentation is half the
          product, because somebody with authority is going to ask to see it.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Pest control use records, kept two years.</strong> Rule 4 TAC §7.144 requires each record
          to contain the customer billing name and address, the service address, the pesticide names
          <em>and their EPA registration numbers</em>, total amounts of ready-to-use pesticide applied, the
          devices used and quantities, the mixing rate and total amount of material applied, the target
          pest or purpose, the date of application, and the applicator's name and licence number along with
          the business licence number. Those records must be made available to the Department on written or
          verbal request.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>That is the document your inspector wants.</strong> Not an invoice showing that somebody
          came. A record showing what was applied, where, at what rate, against what, by whom, on what
          date — and a pattern of that over time demonstrating a programme rather than a reaction. Ask for
          copies for your own file and keep them where you can find them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Invoices carry their own requirements.</strong> Under §7.145(b) the business name,
          licence number, telephone number and address must appear on the face of every invoice, and under
          §7.145(a) contracts and service agreements additionally carry the Department jurisdiction
          statement. Both legible and in at least 8-point type.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The notice regime applies to you twice over.</strong> A restaurant is a workplace under
          §7.146(b) and a food-processing establishment is named explicitly in §7.146(c). We provide the
          sign at least forty-eight hours before a planned indoor treatment; under §7.148(b) you post it in
          an area of common access at least forty-eight hours ahead and make the consumer information sheet
          available to anyone working in the building who asks. Section 7.147(d) adds that the sheet be
          available on request during normal business hours.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A certificate of insurance</strong>, naming your entity, on request — and worth arranging
          before the first visit rather than at the door.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And the wider legal frame.</strong> Texas Health and Safety Code §341.011(3) makes a
          public health nuisance of a restaurant, food market, bakery, other place of business, or vehicle
          in which food is prepared, packed, stored, transported, sold or served to the public that is not
          constantly maintained in a sanitary condition. Pest management is part of what "constantly
          maintained" means in practice.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Rodents in food premises</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Less common than roaches in this sector and far more consequential when it happens.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The entry standard is a quarter inch.</strong> A house mouse can squeeze under a gap
          around a quarter of an inch — AgriLife's school IPM material puts it at openings as small as a
          quarter inch in diameter, and describes a mouse getting through a hole the size of a pencil
          diameter. Rats need slightly more than half an inch. The practical exclusion standard is to seal
          everything larger than a quarter inch, because juveniles are the concern.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Seal with material they cannot chew.</strong> Metal or concrete. Foam, plastic, wood and
          rubber are all gnawable and should not be relied on as a permanent rodent seal — a point New
          Mexico State extension makes explicitly. Quarter-inch hardware cloth for mice, half-inch mesh for
          rats, galvanised sheet metal at 24 gauge or heavier.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>They travel along edges.</strong> The behaviour has a name — thigmotaxis — and AgriLife
          names it: rodents follow linear pathways, the corners between walls and floors, and the base of
          foundations. That is where monitoring and traps go, close enough to the wall that the animal
          passes directly over the trigger, and no more than about ten feet apart in an active area.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Mice work in a small radius.</strong> They seldom venture more than about thirty feet
          from the nest and food supply, returning to the nest up to forty times a day. Which is why mouse
          control is about density of devices in a small area rather than perimeter treatment — and why
          finding the harbourage matters more than covering ground.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Storage practice does a lot of the work.</strong> Food in metal, glass or heavy plastic
          with tight lids. Stored material kept eighteen inches off the floor and away from walls, which is
          both a rodent measure and an inspection one, because you cannot see a runway you cannot get to.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And deliveries are the introduction route</strong> for a great deal of what arrives in
          food premises — rodents, German cockroaches and stored product insects alike. Inspecting incoming
          cartons at the door, and breaking down cardboard rather than storing it long term, closes off
          more problems than any amount of treatment does afterwards.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>How the service runs</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          <strong>Outside operating hours, generally.</strong> Which suits the work as much as the
          business: surfaces clear, equipment movable, crevices reachable, and no notice complications from
          treating occupied space.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The licence holder does the work.</strong> On a food premises that continuity matters
          more than almost anywhere else, because a programme is cumulative — knowing that the gap behind
          the dish machine was sealed in March, and that the monitor by the walk-in has been clean since,
          is what turns visits into a trend.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Monitoring reported rather than assumed.</strong> Sticky-card readings over time are the
          honest measure of whether a programme is working, and they are also the thing that tells you when
          to escalate rather than repeat.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Recurring service with no contract.</strong> Bi-monthly or quarterly, no term, no
          cancellation fee, interim visits between scheduled ones included rather than negotiated. In food
          service the interim call is the whole point — something turns up, you need somebody, and you
          should not be re-negotiating a price to get one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And the honest limits.</strong> No fumigation — structural and commodity fumigation are
          separate licence categories and neither is held here. No bed bug heat treatment. If your
          situation genuinely calls for either, you need a company certified for it and you will be told so
          on the phone rather than after taking the work.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqs} heading="Restaurant pest control questions" />
  <Cta
    line="Describe the roach before anything else — half an inch with two stripes, or an inch and a half and reddish. Those are two different problems and the answer to one is a plumbing job."
  />
</BaseLayout>
